#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4ffa315-2f50-51da-a928-329e1816706f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Why Polygons Are Congruent Using Rigid Motions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d2332-fb73-5b0c-ad16-9c15881f170b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under rotation by ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525215} {:answer/id #uuid "419e2a9c-1e02-5f01-b0c6-b4337088507d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under reflection in the $x$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525368} {:answer/id #uuid "b31b218d-73a7-50ba-b9cf-fe3a8e544a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The polygons are not congruent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525665} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bbbff6-f856-5bda-a992-b2ea7e00eb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under translation by $5$ units to the right and $5$ units down" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525804} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d4e61b-9cb2-5b59-b64f-f3a8558b9a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under translation by $5$ units to the right and $3$ units up" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0bbbff6-f856-5bda-a992-b2ea7e00eb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ is the image of $P$ under translation by $5$ units to the right and $5$ units down" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525804} :answer-field/id #uuid "72cf26f9-bb61-5a25-a7c9-0dc42775de30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fb7b6f3-9396-53a9-a0b3-7b8a7a56bc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-45177" :question/problem "![](images/71/71d77d6d9dda343a95b44fa60afbfee68619aa59bf9c6ba70eb229d072bf9a73.png)\n\nIn terms of rigid motions, why are the polygons $P$ and $Q$ congruent?" :question/worked-solution "The polygons $P$ and $Q$ are congruent because $Q$ is the image of $P$ under the action of translation by $5$ units to the right and $5$ units down.\n\n![](images/15/15120f2beae5043dd439f62bbc376f9b660ec97cf2541c5e2cb28e363ceed202.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b01281fd-5272-579c-98d5-ca5cc00c73e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Maps Presented in Function Notation That Are Rigid Motions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc2d4c0-1618-5fe7-9246-84ee55b59236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525356} {:answer/id #uuid "45ac97e4-9a4a-51c4-8291-5b3046a11d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525382} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1355da-68ee-5967-9be0-84e4e28a596b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525639} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4641ea-e1d1-5f01-ba5e-a6e9674b75ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525751} {:answer/id #uuid "fec35c2b-ba0b-54c0-a2a3-cfc01929dd49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec35c2b-ba0b-54c0-a2a3-cfc01929dd49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525841} :answer-field/id #uuid "f188ddc0-815b-5c23-abd4-3b93e5f115dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a228790b-494e-5666-af68-646e1b5c23fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-45302" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d89477b4c1f7b976f86d1cc5d77c1729826358e1c1367e7982ccd59970c2ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the following combinations of transformations map the triangle $ABC$ to a congruent triangle?\n\nI. A reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by a rotation of ${60}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the point $A.$\n\nII. A translation of $2$ units up, followed by a reflection in the line $\\overset{⟷}{BC}.$\n\nIII. A rotation of ${15}^{∘}$ clockwise about a given point $B,$ followed by a translation of $3$ units up." :question/worked-solution "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n\n- Rotations\n\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Combination I maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a reflection, followed by a rotation) are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination II maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a translation, followed by a reflection) are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination III maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a rotation, followed by a translation) are rigid motions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "811ba45c-7467-5cef-9fb8-a8282891b607" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Rigid Motions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e023e0-beb4-5806-8cfe-d70eb0013d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525347} {:answer/id #uuid "46b79831-e3c1-595b-9cb4-6b62ccc50e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525384} {:answer/id #uuid "804bf539-d1b6-549f-b8a5-0e0cbf6bc49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525524} {:answer/id #uuid "9628c0d6-1a6a-5d22-872b-ffa2a9fa9cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525589} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb2bf95-13d6-556d-aa77-d9bd810bd93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9628c0d6-1a6a-5d22-872b-ffa2a9fa9cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525589} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1af57a5-5f5f-569f-842d-ee65f2687864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e99174b2-b4df-59a0-af23-9fbd3ca32fb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-45606" :question/problem "The polygon ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}{E}^{′}$ is the image of the polygon $ABCDE$ under the transformation $(x,y)↦(y,-x).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $BC={B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\nII. $AB={A}^{′}{B}^{′}.$\n\nIII. $CD<{C}^{′}{D}^{′}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given transformation is a rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin, which is a rigid motion. Rigid motions preserve the distances between all points and all angle measures. \n\n![](images/9b/9b250f6eda245b8a3530851d21c5438ded3fcbe9ba8cbf6db524a2c2749c3d86.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $BC={B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $AB={A}^{′}{B}^{′}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $CD={C}^{′}{D}^{′}.$\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
